{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"**Borrower/default risk:** A borrower may fail to return securities. In the U.S. iShares example, BlackRock’s affiliate arranges loans and requires collateral, but recovery isn’t guaranteed. Controls include borrower limits, collateral haircuts, daily monitoring, and recall rights.\n\n**Collateral-shortfall risk:** Collateral may fall below the cost of replacing loaned securities, causing losses—especially in stressed markets or if liquidation is delayed. Overcollateralization, frequent valuation, margin calls, and diversification help mitigate this.\n\n**Cash-reinvestment risk:** Cash collateral investments may lose value or become illiquid, with losses exceeding income. Guidelines and liquidity, maturity, and credit limits help manage this risk.\n\n**UCITS rules are separate:** UCITS funds generally must be able to recall securities or terminate lending at any time. Non-cash collateral must meet applicable eligibility, valuation, haircut, diversification, and custody requirements. These safeguards mitigate, but don’t eliminate, risk."}}
